COMMAND NAME: mppcreatecluster.sh

Initializes a Greenplum Database system by using configuration parameters specified in
the gp_init_config file of System Initialization Parameters.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mppcreatecluster.sh -c <gp_init_config_file> [-h <host_file>] [-p 
<postgresql_conf_param_file>] [-s <standby_master_host>] [-i] [-o] 
[-m <max_connections>] [-e <superuser_password>] [-n <encoding>] [-a] 
[-q] [-l <logfile_directory>]

mppcreatecluster.sh [-? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mppcreatecluster.sh script will create a Greenplum Database DBMS instance using 
the values defined in a Greenplum Database configuration file. Before running this 
script, make sure that you have installed the Greenplum Database software on all 
the hosts in the array.

The functionality of mppcreatecluster.sh is analogous to PostgreSQLs initdb 
script, which creates the data storage directories, generates the shared 
catalog tables and configuration files, and creates a template database, which 
is a template used to create other databases. 

In a Greenplum Database DBMS, each database instance (the master and all segments) 
must be initialized across all of the hosts in the system in such a way that 
they can all work together as a unified DBMS. The mppcreatecluster.sh script 
takes care of initializing the database on the master and on each segment 
instance, and configuring the system as a whole.

Before running mppcreatecluster.sh, you must set the $MPPHOME or $BIZHOME 
environment variable to point to the location of your Greenplum Database installation 
on the master host.

This script performs the following tasks:

* Verifies that the parameters in the configuration file are correct.

* Ensures that a connection can be established to each host. If a host cannot 
  be reached, the script will exit.

* Displays the configuration that will be used and prompts the user for 
  confirmation.

* Initializes the master instance.

* Initializes the standby master instance (if specified).

* Initializes the primary segment instances.

* Initializes the mirror segment instances (if mirroring is configured).

* Configures the Greenplum Database system and checks for errors.

* Starts the Greenplum Database system.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-c <gp_init_config_file>

Required. The full path and filename of the configuration file, which 
contains all of the defined parameters to configure the array.

-h <host_file>

Optional. The full path and filename of a file that contains the host names 
of all the segment hosts in the array. If not specified on the command line, 
you can specify the host file using the MACHINE_LIST_FILE parameter in the 
gp_init_config file.

-p <postgresql_conf_param_file>

Optional. The name of a file that contains global postgresql.conf parameter 
settings that you want to set for Greenplum Database. These settings will be used 
when the individual master and segment instances are initialized.

-s <standby_master_host>

Optional. If you wish to configure a backup master host, specify the host name 
using this option. You must have the Greenplum Database software installed and 
configured on the backup master host.

-i

If a standby master host is configured, this will stop the synchronization 
process between the primary and backup master hosts. The default is to 
start the synchronization process.

-o

Creates a master instance only. Does not configure any segment hosts.

-m <max_connections>

Sets the maximum number of client connections allowed to the master. The 
default is 25.

-e <superuser_password>

The password to set for the Greenplum Database database super user. Defaults to 
'mpparray'. You can always change the super user password at a later time 
using the ALTER ROLE command. Client connections over the network require 
a password login for the database super user account (for example, the 
bgadmin user).

-n <encoding>

Sets the default character set encoding for the Greenplum Database system. Defaults to 
UNICODE. See Character Set Support in the PostgreSQL documentation for more 
information on the supported character set encodings.

-a

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.

-q

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but is still 
written to the log file.

-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/mppAdminLogs.

-?

Displays the online help.

-v

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Initialize a Greenplum Database array:

mppcreatecluster.sh -c gp_init_config -e mypassword


Initialize a Greenplum Database array with an optional standby master host:

mppcreatecluster.sh -c gp_init_config -s host09 -e mypassword


Display the online help for the mppcreatecluster.sh script:

mppcreatecluster.sh -?


